{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/9/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5adee1b2\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nUse the left-edge color legend to draw a padded rectangular halo around each matching object while preserving enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- One or more two-row markers sit on the left edge; each marker is a 2x2 block whose left column is an object color and right column is its halo color.\n- Away from the markers, every source color from the legend appears as one or more 4-connected monochrome objects.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input pixels unchanged.\n- For each source-color object, take its tight bbox expanded by one cell and clipped to the grid.\n- Recolor background pixels in that expanded region to the marker's halo color, except for background holes enclosed by the object.\n\nKey insight:\n- The small left-edge bars are a legend: they map object colors to the colors used for the surrounding padded bbox.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5623160b\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nPush every colored object touching maroon markers straight outward until it reaches the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Maroon pixels are fixed markers.\n- Every other foreground 4-connected object touches the marker pixels on exactly one cardinal side.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each non-background, non-maroon object from its original position.\n- Translate it in the direction away from its maroon contact until the object reaches the grid boundary.\n- Keep the maroon marker pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The maroon contact side is a launcher: it determines a unique outward ray for the whole object, and the edge of the grid determines the translation distance.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3dc255db\" lines=\"37-53\">\n<description>\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9841fdad\" lines=\"15-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the colored runs from the left reference panel into the blank right panel.\n\nInput structure:\n- A uniform rectangular frame surrounds the grid.\n- One full-height divider of the frame color splits a left reference panel\n  from a blank right panel.\n- Both panels have one background margin column on each side of their active\n  columns; the left active columns contain two-column endpoint slots, possibly\n  connected by same-color horizontal runs.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the original grid and paint only in the right panel's active columns.\n- A source run exactly matching the left endpoint slot is copied to the left\n  two columns of the right active span.\n- A source run exactly matching the right endpoint slot is copied to the right\n  two columns of the right active span.\n- A source run connecting the endpoint slots is stretched across the entire\n  right active span.\n\nKey insight:\nThe left panel is a compressed legend for the right panel: endpoint markers\nstay two pixels wide, while connectors between endpoints expand to fill the\ndistance between them.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5ad8a7c0\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nConnect the innermost mirrored marker pair(s) by filling their horizontal span.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Each foreground row contains exactly two foreground pixels mirrored across the vertical center.\n\nRule\n- Find the foreground row pair(s) with the smallest horizontal gap between the two markers.\n- Fill every pixel between those two markers in each selected row with the foreground color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Wider marker pairs are context; the closest marker pair(s) show the unresolved horizontal segment.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1efba499\" lines=\"70-85\">\n<description>\nSwap paired marker pixels across the largest foreground-color barrier, placing them just outside the barrier segment.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color is the unique most frequent non-background color; its pixels form the stationary barrier.\n- Other foreground pixels are single markers, sometimes paired on opposite sides of one contiguous barrier segment in the same row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Scan each row and column that crosses exactly one contiguous barrier segment.\n- If the line has exactly one marker on each side of that segment, erase both source markers and place each marker in the cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the segment.\n- Lines with markers on only one side are unchanged; the barrier remains unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker pairing is local to a row or column, and the destination is defined by the nearest cells outside the intervening barrier segment, not by the original distance from the barrier.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bd283c4a\" lines=\"7-23\">\n<description>\nPack all pixels into color-frequency runs along bottom-up columns.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is a rectangular grid whose colors are all foreground material;\nonly the number of pixels of each color matters. In the observed task\ninstances, every present color has a distinct pixel count.\n\nRule\nCount the pixels of each color and order the colors from most frequent to\nleast frequent. Rewrite a same-size grid by placing each color's full count\nas one contiguous run while scanning columns from left to right and, within\neach column, from bottom to top.\n\nKey insight\nThe output preserves the multiset of colors but discards all original\npositions, turning the grid into a frequency-sorted column packing.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"25c199f5\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nPack the non-background rows from three separated panels into one panel, scanning panels right-to-left.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid contains three equal-size panels separated by two full-height divider columns.\n- The background is the dominant color; divider pixels use a color that appears only in the divider columns.\n\nRule:\n- Read panels from right to left, and within each panel read rows from top to bottom.\n- Keep each row that contains any non-background pixel, discarding all-background rows.\n- Place the kept rows at the bottom of a single panel-shaped output, padding above with background rows.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is row packing: the divider columns mark independent panels, and only the occupied rows are moved.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5b37cb25\" lines=\"59-73\">\n<description>\nFill each plus-shaped background notch with the color of the frame side it opens toward.\n\nInput structure\n- A one-cell-thick rectangular frame has four solid-colored sides and neutral corner pixels.\n- The frame interior contains a dominant background color and one foreground object color.\n- Some object boundaries contain five-pixel plus-shaped background notches.\n\nRule\n- Find every background plus whose opposite side is supported by foreground object pixels and whose open side faces one frame side.\n- Recolor the five pixels of that plus with the color of the facing frame side.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nThe frame side colors are direction labels for the open notches in the foreground object.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"320afe60\" lines=\"41-56\">\n<description>\nSlide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.\n\nInput structure:\n- Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.\n\nRule:\n- If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green.\n- Otherwise slide the object to the left edge and recolor it red.\n- Start from a blank background canvas; no moved objects overlap.\n\nKey insight:\n- The target side is encoded by the object's shape, not by its original position: open notches go right, while closed or solid shapes go left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"20270e3b\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nConnect two sprites using matching connector pixels, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background color `bg`, sprite color `obj`, connector color `ctrl` (small, 2 components).\n- Two main foreground sprites, plus possibly tiny detached pixels belonging to one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the **base** sprite (closest to grid top-left).\n- Translate the other sprite so its connector sits one cell above the base's connector.\n- Stamp the plug onto the base; remove connectors (recolor `ctrl` to `obj`).\n- Output is the tight crop around all non-background pixels.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
